\section{Motivation}
\label{sec:motivation}

\subsection{Problem Statement}
\label{subsec:problem_statement}

As telecommunications networks move toward 6G, recent AI-RAN work frames the RAN as more than a communications substrate: it is increasingly presented as a cloud-native, distributed platform that can host both primary Radio Access Network (RAN) functions and selected AI workloads on shared infrastructure \cite{beyond,airan,openairan}. A compelling use case within this convergence is latency-sensitive edge inference, where placing model execution close to the radio path can reduce transport delay and keep demanding AI workloads off user devices \cite{beyond,cora,slaairan}.

RAN infrastructure is also provisioned for peak demand, which leaves meaningful spare capacity outside worst-case conditions \cite{beyond,airan}. Recent profiling from \textit{Weaver} makes this point concrete: even in a pessimistic four-DU peak-load setting, about 40\% spare compute remains, and production-trace replays show that spare SM capacity can still be around 85\% during peak daytime periods across six cell sites from three operators \cite{weaver}. The same study shows that this headroom is bursty within a site but complementary across sites, which makes it valuable yet difficult to harvest with static sharing policies \cite{weaver}.

The central challenge, then, is safe coexistence between workloads with very different timing contracts. DU processing follows tight slot-level deadlines, while edge inference must meet service latency targets without degrading the primary radio function \cite{beyond,weaver,cora}. Existing GPU-sharing mechanisms expose the trade-off: hardware partitioning such as NVIDIA's Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) offers strong isolation and is therefore a useful hard-isolation option or comparison baseline, but static partitions are a poor fit for bursty millisecond-scale spare cycles; more elastic sharing can expose more headroom, but makes deadline protection harder to guarantee \cite{weaver,slaairan}. This creates a clear need for a scheduling architecture that keeps a strict ``RAN-first'' policy while still exposing usable spare compute to multi-tenant edge AI services.


\subsection{Research Hypothesis and Objectives}
\label{subsec:hypothesis_objectives}

\subsubsection{Research Hypothesis}
Recent profiling of accelerated RAN infrastructure, especially in \textit{Weaver}, shows why AI \textit{training} is a comparatively favorable co-located workload: LDPC-heavy RAN processing is bandwidth-dominated, while foundation-model training is more compute-dominated, giving the two workloads a degree of resource complementarity \cite{weaver}. \textit{Weaver} also shows that stabilizing bursty spare compute can raise training throughput from about 54 TFLOPs to about 142 TFLOPs under similar average SM availability, highlighting how strongly workload performance depends on the temporal shape of spare capacity \cite{weaver}.

Deploying AI \textit{inference} is harder because inference is not a single resource profile. Request transport, model execution, and response delivery stress different parts of the system, and the most latency-sensitive phases leave much less room for contention with primary RAN functions \cite{cora,weaver,slaairan}. Furthermore, enabling this as an edge service requires multi-tenancy. \textit{Paradrop} showed that lightweight, containerized services can be placed at the extreme edge, but it did not need the sub-millisecond priority handling expected in a DU-adjacent RAN environment \cite{paradrop}.

Therefore, the core \textbf{Research Hypothesis} of this proposal is:
\textit{By employing a hierarchical scheduling architecture that combines a slow-timescale, cloud-native orchestration layer for multi-tenant isolation with a fast-timescale, ``RAN-first'' local MAC scheduler, it is possible to dynamically multiplex latency-critical AI inference workloads onto the spare compute capacity of GPU-accelerated RAN Distributed Units (DUs). We hypothesize that this architecture can satisfy the real-time latency bounds of edge inference while guaranteeing zero Service Level Agreement (SLA) violations for primary telecommunications workloads.}

\subsubsection{Objectives}
To test this hypothesis and realize the proposed architecture, this research will pursue the following specific objectives, categorized by architectural layer:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Design a Cloud-Native, Multi-Tenant Orchestration Layer (Macro-level):}
	      Extend extreme-edge orchestration principles, such as those explored in \textit{Paradrop}, to the 6G RAN environment. This involves designing a slow-timescale (seconds to minutes) management plane responsible for tenant onboarding, containerized workload isolation, and SLA-class assignment across the O-Cloud \cite{paradrop,beyond,openairan}.

	\item \textbf{Develop a ``RAN-first'' Micro-Scheduler (Micro-level):}
	      Design a fast-timescale (sub-10 millisecond) scheduling algorithm residing at the DU. This scheduler will dynamically allocate bursty spare GPU cycles to inference tasks and compare hard-isolation options such as MIG against more elastic sharing mechanisms to determine which choices best protect baseband deadlines while still exposing useful spare capacity \cite{weaver,slaairan}.

	\item \textbf{Implement End-to-End Latency Coordination:}
	      Adapt the end-to-end budgeting logic exemplified by \textit{CORA} so that overall service delay is apportioned across uplink transport, edge execution, and downlink delivery, with the compute budget refined further when different inference phases exhibit different runtime behavior \cite{cora}.

	\item \textbf{System Evaluation and Profiling:}
	      Evaluate the proposed hierarchical architecture within a simulated or physical AI-RAN testbed. The primary metrics for success will be demonstrating 0\% SLA violations for the primary RAN workload under heavy load, alongside acceptable inference latency and increased overall GPU utilization.
\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Timeliness, Novelty, and Significance}
\label{subsec:timeliness_novelty}

\subsubsection{Timeliness}
The timing is favorable because AI-RAN is moving from broad vision to concrete, cloud-native architectural proposals and deployment workflows \cite{beyond,airan,openairan}. At the same time, large-scale RAN experimentation and latency-critical cellular inference are becoming more concrete through public-cloud emulation platforms such as \textit{CHRONOS} and end-to-end coordination frameworks such as \textit{CORA} \cite{chronos,cora}. Recent device-RAN-cloud inference work further suggests that SLA-aware placement is becoming a practical systems question rather than a purely conceptual one \cite{slaairan}.

\subsubsection{Novelty and Relation to Prior Work}
Prior work addresses important pieces of this problem, but not the full combination required here. \textit{Beyond Connectivity} and related AI-RAN architecture efforts describe the orchestration substrate for shared AI and RAN infrastructure \cite{beyond,airan,openairan}. \textit{Paradrop} demonstrates lightweight multi-tenancy at the extreme edge \cite{paradrop}. \textit{CORA} coordinates uplink, compute, and downlink for latency-critical inference \cite{cora}. \textit{Weaver} shows how to expose RAN-safe spare GPU capacity, but its target workload is foundation model training rather than latency-critical inference \cite{weaver}. \textit{SLA-Aware Distributed LLM Inference Across Device-RAN-Cloud} further suggests that strict SLAs may require hard isolation or carefully selected model variants, which makes MIG a useful comparison point rather than a universal answer \cite{slaairan}.

The novelty of this research lies in combining these strands into one scheduling problem: determining which inference workloads can share DU-adjacent compute, under what isolation contract, and with what end-to-end latency budget. The project therefore focuses on phase-aware inference profiling, DU-local coexistence control, and multi-tenant orchestration that joins \textit{Weaver}-style spare-compute harvesting with \textit{CORA}-style latency coordination and \textit{Paradrop}-style lifecycle management \cite{weaver,cora,paradrop}.

\subsubsection{Significance}
This research has the potential to fundamentally alter the economics and operational capabilities of next-generation telecommunications. By proving that multi-tenant edge inference can be executed natively on RAN infrastructure with zero degradation to network reliability, this work unlocks a ubiquitous, ``free'' compute substrate for AI services. For network operators, it provides a blueprint to monetize idle GPU cycles without deploying new hardware. For end-users and device manufacturers, it guarantees the low-latency edge compute necessary to power lightweight, battery-efficient mobile devices. Ultimately, this research provides a critical technical foundation for making AI services an integral, native component of 6G networks.

