\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:background_related_work}

This section provides the foundational context necessary to understand the proposed scheduling architecture. It first outlines the evolution of cloud-native telecommunications and edge AI, before critically reviewing the existing literature on GPU sharing, edge multi-tenancy, and RAN-aware scheduling to highlight the specific gaps this research will address.

\subsection{Background}
\label{subsec:background}

\subsubsection{Cloud-Native O-RAN Architecture}
Historically, cellular network deployments relied heavily on monolithic, proprietary hardware appliances characterized by strict vendor lock-in and rigid operational paradigms. The Open RAN (O-RAN) standard initiated a fundamental shift in this ecosystem by promoting the disaggregation of traditional baseband functions and the adoption of standardized, open interfaces \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: open\&airan.pdf}. A central tenet of the O-RAN specification is the defined functional split—most notably Split 7.2—which cleanly divides Physical (PHY) layer processing \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: open\&airan.pdf}. Under this architecture, the lower PHY functions reside at the Radio Unit (O-RU), while the computationally intensive upper PHY operations are handled by the Distributed Unit (O-DU) \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: open\&airan.pdf}.

Crucially, O-RAN transitions these disaggregated components away from specialized telecommunications equipment toward a highly flexible, cloud-native architecture \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: beyond.pdf}. In a virtualized RAN (vRAN) deployment, network functions are completely decoupled from the underlying physical hardware and are instead deployed as containerized microservices \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: beyond.pdf}. These virtualized workloads run on Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) servers, which collectively form the computing substrate known as the O-Cloud. To manage this distributed environment, the architecture introduces the Service Management and Orchestration (SMO) framework \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: beyond.pdf}. The SMO leverages standard IT cloud orchestration principles to handle the lifecycle management, dynamic scaling, and deployment of virtualized network functions across the O-Cloud, ensuring multi-vendor interoperability and programmatic control \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: beyond.pdf}. Furthermore, O-RAN integrates AI/ML-driven network automation into the architecture, enabling intelligent orchestration of both computational and communication resources \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: AIRAN.pdf}.

However, realizing this cloud-native flexibility introduces severe execution challenges, primarily due to the stringent, deterministic nature of cellular telecommunications. RAN functions demand substantial compute and network resources. The network operates in a strict time-slotted fashion, with slot durations ranging from hundreds of microseconds to just one millisecond \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CHRONOS.pdf}. During a single slot, the system must execute radio resource allocation in the control plane and process user packets in the data plane (e.g., segmentation, header attachment, and retransmissions) \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CHRONOS.pdf}.

Executing these slot-level tasks on standard cloud infrastructure is demanding. CHRONOS shows that VM interruption and network latency can consume a large share of the slot budget, so faithful cloud execution depends on tight release synchrony and careful control of timing jitter \cite{chronos}. This timing pressure helps explain why recent O-RAN and AI-RAN work places disaggregated DU/CU software, O-Cloud resources, and accelerator support within a common cloud-native operating model, rather than treating real-time RAN functions as fixed-purpose appliances \cite{openairan,beyond,airan}.


\subsubsection{The AI-RAN Paradigm}
As cellular systems move toward 6G, the RAN is increasingly framed as both a communications system and a distributed compute platform. The AI-RAN literature describes this convergence as a joint evolution of infrastructure, control, and services, where AI capabilities are embedded into the RAN stack and coordinated across edge and cloud resources \cite{beyond,airan,openairan}.

The recent literature organizes this convergence into three closely related pillars \cite{airan,openairan}:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{AI-for-RAN (Network Optimization):} This pillar applies AI to improve internal network functions, including scheduling, beam management, traffic steering, energy saving, and anomaly handling \cite{airan,openairan}. Here, AI remains subordinate to the primary telecommunications service.
	\item \textbf{AI-on-RAN (Native AI Services):} This pillar treats the RAN and its edge compute continuum as a native platform for AI services, especially distributed learning and low-latency inference placed close to DU/CU resources \cite{airan}.
	\item \textbf{AI-and-RAN (Infrastructure Multiplexing):} This pillar studies shared-infrastructure coexistence, asking when spare O-Cloud or AI-RAN compute can host secondary AI workloads without breaking primary radio deadlines \cite{airan,weaver,beyond}.
\end{itemize}

This research proposal focuses strictly on the \textbf{AI-and-RAN} pillar. Relative to the broader AI-for-RAN literature, it remains at an earlier stage of system design and validation, even though it offers a clear economic reason to reuse AI-RAN infrastructure beyond peak radio demand periods \cite{airan,weaver,beyond}.

In practice, that reuse depends on turning spare DU or O-Cloud compute into a controlled edge service substrate while preserving the primacy of RAN timing and reliability requirements \cite{beyond,weaver,chronos}. The central systems question is therefore not whether AI workloads can be injected in the abstract, but how to admit and coordinate only those workloads that remain compatible with the underlying radio deadlines \cite{airan,weaver}.


\subsubsection{The Anatomy of Latency-Critical Inference Workloads}
Latency-sensitive edge AI workloads are also part of this background. CORA models their end-to-end service time as a joint uplink, compute, and downlink problem, while recent device-RAN-cloud measurements show that strict edge feasibility depends on workload variant and placement rather than on accelerator presence alone \cite{cora,slaairan}.

As a result, edge inference is a multi-resource scheduling problem whose bottleneck can shift with channel quality, traffic load, and input/output size \cite{cora}. For generative services, this difficulty is amplified by the fact that different stages of inference place different demands on the platform \cite{slaairan}.

The first stage is the \textbf{Prefill Phase} (or prompt processing). In this stage, the model processes the prompt in parallel and establishes the time-to-first-token budget. This phase is typically more compute-heavy than later generation, which is why TTFT becomes a useful operational signal under strict edge SLAs \cite{slaairan}.

The second stage is the \textbf{Autoregressive Decoding Phase}. In this phase, the model generates the output sequence sequentially, one token at a time. To predict the next token, the model must retrieve the historical context from the KV Cache. Because the arithmetic intensity of generating a single token is remarkably low compared to the size of the model, the GPU's compute cores often sit idle waiting for data \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: SLA.pdf}. Therefore, the decoding phase is severely \textit{memory-bandwidth bound}. For every single generated token, the entire model weight matrices must be transferred from High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) to the Streaming Multiprocessors (SMs), heavily taxing the L2 cache and memory bus \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: SLA.pdf}. The efficiency of this phase is measured by Token Throughput (TPT).

This two-phase dichotomy represents the fundamental challenge of multiplexing AI inference with RAN workloads. Standard IT schedulers rely on ALU utilization metrics to identify ``spare compute.'' During the autoregressive decoding phase, an LLM exhibits low ALU utilization, falsely signaling to a naive scheduler that the GPU is available for concurrent tasks. However, injecting a latency-critical RAN baseband workload during this phase will trigger catastrophic contention over the L2 cache and memory bus, inevitably leading to RAN SLA violations and dropped physical layer deadlines \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CHRONOS.pdf, SLA.pdf}.

Empirical evaluations on AI-RAN testbeds reveal that SLA feasibility for inference is further heavily dominated by the choice of model variant \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: SLA.pdf}. For example, quantized models (which reduce memory precision) are often necessary to reliably meet sub-0.5-second deadlines, whereas unquantized models frequently incur severe deadline misses due to GPU stalls and queueing delays under multi-tenant contention \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: SLA.pdf}.

Furthermore, inference workloads do not operate in a vacuum; their latency is a product of an end-to-end (E2E) pipeline. The total inference delay is the sum of the time required to upload input data (Uplink), execute the model on the GPU (Compute), and transmit the generated results back to the user (Downlink) \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CORA.pdf}. The dominant bottleneck within this pipeline dynamically shifts depending on the workload \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: edge\_ran\_inference\_research\_matrix.md}. For instance, a workload with high-resolution video inputs will heavily stress the uplink Radio Resource Blocks (RBs), while an LLM with minimal text input but complex generation will dominate the SMs on the GPU \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CORA.pdf}. Furthermore, channel quality acts as a first-order constraint; poor RF conditions can artificially inflate the uplink time, starving the GPU of data and destroying the latency budget \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: edge\_ran\_inference\_research\_matrix.md}.

Finally, the traffic dynamics of inference workloads severely complicate resource allocation. Unlike smooth, predictable data streams, multi-tenant inference traffic is highly idiosyncratic. It is characterized by sequence-length variance, unaligned request arrivals, and bursty interactive sessions \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: edge\_ran\_inference\_research\_matrix.md}. These dynamics create highly variable queueing behaviors, making edge inference exceptionally susceptible to tail-latencies. Consequently, any system attempting to co-schedule inference alongside primary RAN workloads must possess the micro-level agility to preempt hardware resources dynamically while managing the macro-level constraints of the E2E pipeline.


\subsubsection{Strict RAN Service Level Agreements (SLAs) vs. IT Workloads}
Sharing this cloud-native hardware introduces a severe clash of operational philosophies. In standard IT cloud environments, schedulers optimize for fairness. Conversely, the RAN operates under unforgiving Service Level Agreements (SLAs). The network operates in a time-slotted fashion, with each slot having a duration in the order of hundreds of microseconds up to a millisecond \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CHRONOS.pdf}. During each slot, the MAC scheduler must perform radio resource allocation for both uplink and downlink, and the data plane must process the actual packets (segmentation, adding headers, retransmissions) \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CHRONOS.pdf}. Both control and data plane operations must conclude within the exact slot duration; failure to do so results in packet drops, user detaches, or catastrophic RAN crashes \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CHRONOS.pdf}. Therefore, coexistence requires a strict ``RAN-first'' priority model where the telecommunications workload is unconditionally protected.


\subsubsection{The Hardware Dichotomy: Training vs. Inference}
Within the AI domain, different workloads stress the GPU in fundamentally different ways. Foundation model training is one of the most compute-intensive workloads available \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: weaver.pdf}. However, training is elastic; it is a background task that can adapt to dynamic resource availability and be safely paused when RAN traffic spikes \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: weaver.pdf}. Inference presents a radically different hardware profile. Edge inference is inherently user-centric, interactive, and latency-critical \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CORA.pdf}. Furthermore, LLM and Vision-Language Model (VLM) inference is heavily memory-bound, highly sensitive to cache contention, and requires strict SLA latency budgets \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: SLA.pdf}. A scheduling architecture designed to safely multiplex elastic training workloads cannot be naively applied to inference.


\subsection{Related Work}
\label{subsec:related_work}

\subsubsection{Hardware-Level GPU Sharing Mechanisms}
To realize AI-and-RAN, the GPU must be shared between the RAN DU and the LLM inference engine. Existing GPU sharing mechanisms fail to meet RAN's stringent SLAs. Spatial partitioning techniques, such as NVIDIA's Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) evaluated in systems like \textit{CAORA}, provide excellent workload isolation but force static resource allocation and incur massive reconfiguration penalties ($\sim$7 seconds) \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: weaver.pdf}. This makes them too rigid to capture bursty spare compute. Conversely, temporal sharing mechanisms like Multi-Process Service (MPS), evaluated in \textit{YinYangRAN}, allow dynamic sharing but incur a non-negligible reconfiguration overhead ($\sim$0.3 seconds) during which the GPU is not usable and all workloads must fall back to CPUs \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: weaver.pdf}. Neither approach provides the sub-millisecond hardware preemption required to guarantee RAN baseband processing priority.

\subsubsection{Edge Multi-Tenancy and Orchestration}
Providing AI inference as a multi-tenant service requires robust macro-level orchestration. Foundational systems like \textit{Paradrop} successfully demonstrated how to enable lightweight multi-tenancy at the network's extreme edge by deploying isolated, containerized environments \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: edge\_ran\_inference\_research\_matrix.md}. While Paradrop validates the cloud-to-edge multi-tenancy concept, cellular 6G architectures propose extending the O-RAN Service Management and Orchestration (SMO) layer into an ``AI-RAN Orchestrator'' to enable integrated resource allocation across RAN and AI workloads \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: beyond.pdf}. However, these high-level orchestration planes must be paired with low-level execution engines to guarantee isolation on shared GPUs.

\subsubsection{End-to-End Latency Coordination}
Inference latency encompasses the time required to upload data (Uplink), process it (Compute), and return the result (Downlink) \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CORA.pdf}. In the cellular domain, the \textit{CORA} framework advanced this concept by demonstrating end-to-end latency coordination. CORA explicitly models separate uplink, compute, and downlink latency budgets, deriving Resource Block (RB) demand from I/O size and channel quality, and Streaming Multiprocessor (SM) demand from a speedup model \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: edge\_ran\_inference\_research\_matrix.md}. It dynamically adjusts these budgets across stages based on each DNN task’s characteristics to mitigate contention \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: CORA.pdf}. However, while CORA excellently manages macro-level coordination, it abstracts away the micro-level hardware contention on the base station's GPU.

\subsubsection{RAN-Aware Co-scheduling}
Addressing micro-level hardware contention, the \textit{Weaver} framework represents the state-of-the-art in RAN-safe GPU sharing. Weaver introduced a RAN-centric spare compute controller embedded directly inside the MAC scheduler, utilizing NVIDIA Green Contexts to achieve sub-millisecond elasticity without violating RAN deadlines \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: weaver.pdf}. Their analysis revealed that GPU-accelerated RAN systems possess substantial underutilized compute resources (40–85\%) that are temporally bursty within a single cell site \todo[inline, color=orange!40]{Cite: weaver.pdf}. However, Weaver was explicitly designed and evaluated for foundation model training.


\subsection{Summary of Research Gap}
\label{subsec:research_gap}
The synthesis of the current literature reveals a distinct architectural gap. While cloud-native O-RAN provides the infrastructure, frameworks like CORA solve macro-level latency routing, and Paradrop enables containerized edge multi-tenancy, there is no existing micro-level scheduling solution capable of safely multiplexing \textit{latency-critical inference} onto shared RAN GPUs. Because LLM inference is highly memory-bound and cannot be arbitrarily paused like training workloads, it requires a novel scheduling approach. This research bridges this gap by adapting Weaver's sub-millisecond elasticity protocols to accommodate the strict latency bounds and memory profiles of edge inference, integrated within a multi-tenant orchestration pipeline.


%==========================================================
% Chapter 3 -> Section 3
\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

To test the core hypothesis and develop the proposed multi-tenant AI-RAN architecture, this research will adopt a phased, systems-engineering methodology.

\textbf{Phase 1: Workload Profiling and Contention Analysis.}\\
Before a scheduler can be designed, the exact resource demands of both workloads must be established. We will profile standard 5G DU baseband processing alongside a suite of edge AI models (LLMs/VLMs) to establish their precise compute-bound versus memory-bound characteristics. Co-locating these workloads will identify exactly which hardware resources (L2 Cache, memory bandwidth, PCIe bus) cause RAN SLA violations.

\textbf{Phase 2: Micro-Level Scheduler Design.}\\
Based on profiling data, a dynamic MAC-layer scheduler will be developed. Instead of relying on static partitioning, it will adapt NVIDIA Green Contexts and sub-millisecond preemption protocols to safely inject memory-bound inference tasks into RAN idle slots without compromising the primary telecommunication deadline.

\textbf{Phase 3: Macro-Level Orchestration Integration.}\\
The scheduler will be integrated into a broader cloud-native framework to achieve multi-tenancy. This involves extending standard orchestration principles (such as Kubernetes pods or Paradrop-style isolated environments) to handle tenant isolation and implement the decoupled end-to-end latency planning mechanism.


